{"agent":"architecture","findings":[{"id":"arch-001","file":"triggarr/clients/base.py","line":233,"title":"validate_connection HTTP-error log drops the codebase's established sanitized-exception pattern and logs raw exc","category":"pattern-consistency","cwe":null,"severity":"medium","agent_confidence":88,"in_diff":true,"intent_doc_match":null,"problem":"The codebase has a standard way to log httpx/pydantic exceptions: reduce them to a type-based summary, never str(exc). There are three examples of it. `_sanitize_exc` in triggarr/search/engine.py:30, whose docstring says raw str(exc) 'may contain internal paths, URLs, or API keys that bypass the loguru redacting sink', is used at engine.py:366/619/881/1138/1274/1413 and routes.py:968/1073. The same inline `f"HTTP {exc.response.status_code}"` summary appears in this file at base.py:71-75 (the _request_with_retry warning 160 lines above) and in tracking.py:71. This hunk goes back to passing the raw HTTPStatusError. Its str() includes the full request URL and httpx's help-link text. This line is now the only httpx-error log in the clients layer that skips the convention. The diff exactly reverses commit d47b4c2 ('fix(security): prevent potential key leakage in validate_connection log messages').","current_code":"logger.warning(
                    "{app}: Unexpected HTTP error: {exc}",
                    app=self._app_name,
                    exc=exc,
                )","fix_hint":"Restore status=exc.response.status_code. Or better, move _sanitize_exc to a shared module such as triggarr/clients/errors.py and call it here, in _request_with_retry and in tracking.py.","why_it_matters":"This quietly undoes a deliberate security hardening, and the codebase's sanitize convention exists to keep URLs and secrets out of logs. Logs also go into an in-memory buffer that the web UI shows (logging.py:71). The redacting sink only catches exact API-key strings. It does not catch other URL contents, such as credentials embedded in a configured base_url.","silenced_marker_nearby":false},{"id":"arch-002","file":"triggarr/clients/base.py","line":252,"title":"validate_connection ValidationError log replaces error_count summary with raw exc, against the established sanitize convention","category":"pattern-consistency","cwe":null,"severity":"medium","agent_confidence":85,"in_diff":true,"intent_doc_match":null,"problem":"`_sanitize_exc` in triggarr/search/engine.py:43 shows how pydantic errors should be logged: `f"validation error ({exc.error_count()} issues)"`. It is used across engine.py and routes.py, and d47b4c2 brought this site into line with it. The diff switches back to str(ValidationError). That output includes `input_value=` fragments of the remote *arr response body and pydantic doc URLs, so data from the third-party API ends up verbatim in the logs.","current_code":"logger.warning(
                "{app}: Unexpected API response format: {exc}",
                app=self._app_name,
                exc=exc,
            )","fix_hint":"Restore count=exc.error_count(), or call a shared _sanitize_exc helper.","why_it_matters":"Remote response content leaks into logs that are stored and shown in the UI. It also breaks the rule this codebase follows elsewhere that exceptions are summarized before logging.","silenced_marker_nearby":false}],"agent_notes":["The whole diff is a line-for-line reversal of commit d47b4c2 (fix(security): prevent potential key leakage in validate_connection log messages). Unless there is an intent doc that authorizes reverting it, this looks like an accidental regression. Possible causes are a stale stash or a cherry-pick from the pre-rename fetcharr history (b8125c8 had this same code).","No tests in tests/ assert on these log messages, so the regression would pass `pytest` silently. A caplog test on validate_connection's non-401 and ValidationError branches would lock in the sanitized form.","A second copy of the sanitize logic already exists inline (base.py:71-75 and tracking.py:71 duplicate part of engine._sanitize_exc). That makes three or more sites, so under the rule of three, moving `_sanitize_exc` out of search/engine.py into a shared module (e.g. triggarr/clients/errors.py) is justified. routes.py already imports it from engine.py, which is a slightly odd web→search dependency for a generic utility.","The security implications (CWE-532, info exposure through logs) belong to the security agent. I filed these as pattern-consistency because the architectural problem is moving away from an established convention with named call sites.","Files reviewed: /Users/julianamacbook/triggarr/triggarr/clients/base.py, /Users/julianamacbook/triggarr/triggarr/search/engine.py (lines 30-44)."]}
